WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.660
On-set naps, six shots of espresso, and lots
of O'Doyles — it's no wonder Phillip Seymour

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.060
Hoffman gave "Billy Madison" a pass.

00:00:09.216 --> 00:00:14.850
In another universe, the talented Bob Odenkirk
of "Better Call Saul" and "Breaking Bad" fame

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.900
would have become a superstar much earlier
in his career.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:23.300
Alas, while the comedy writer received plenty
of acclaim and goodwill for his stints on

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.949
"Mr. Show" and "The Ben Stiller Show," chances
are his Hollywood ascent might have gone quite

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:32.460
differently had the executives at Universal
Studios listened to Sandler.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.790
Sandler wanted Odenkirk to play the villainous
Eric Gordon, a role eventually assumed by

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:38.120
Bradley Whitford.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.820
At the time, Odenkirk was mainly known for
his work behind the scenes at "Saturday Night

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.790
Live," where he wrote the famed "Motivational
Speaker" sketch for Chris Farley that only

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.870
aired after he left the gig.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.760
Sandler revealed on the "Fly on the Wall"
podcast that the studio didn't want to simply

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:58.250
fill the cast with friends of the comedian,
and wouldn't allow him to cast Odenkirk in the role.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.680
When Sandler's efforts to bring in Odenkirk
failed, he pivoted in a different direction

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.920
after viewing a hilarious audition tape from
then up-and-coming actor Philip Seymour Hoffman.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.659
In the same interview with Dana Carvey and
David Spade on "Fly On the Wall", Sandler

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:17.280
revealed that the studio held open auditions
following the Odenkirk rejection.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.030
Sandler caught a glimpse of Hoffman and was
absolutely blown away by what he saw.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:25.329
Sandler went to the studio and begged them
to hire the man.

00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:30.219
After much deliberation, they finally relented
and agreed - only for Hoffman to turn down

00:01:30.219 --> 00:01:31.259
the part.

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:34.210
Sandler was stunned and decided to call Hoffman
himself.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:38.679
After explaining how much he liked his audition,
for which Hoffman politely thanked him, Sandler

00:01:38.679 --> 00:01:42.030
then said that he had been told Hoffman didn't
want the part.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.180
Asked by Sandler why he didn't want to do
it, Hoffman simply replied -

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.649
"Awww, I just don't want to."

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:52.130
Hoffman, of course, would go on to have a
terrific career that saw him win numerous

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:57.090
accolades, and was even able to eventually
work alongside Sandler in "Punch Drunk Love"

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.212
before tragically passing away in 2014.

00:02:00.212 --> 00:02:04.810
One of the funniest scenes in "Billy Madison"
involves the titular character taking part

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.549
in a game of dodgeball with a bunch of first
graders.

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:12.340
In his first go, the young kids, led by a
member of the dreaded O'Doyle clan, knocks

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:16.269
the grown man out of the game before he can
even reach the playground.

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:20.599
After receiving encouragement from his teacher,
Billy gives it another go and decides to let

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:23.049
loose and exact some brutal revenge.

00:02:23.049 --> 00:02:27.560
As the actor recalled on "Conan," during the
scene he hit a couple kids so hard with the

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.730
ball that he actually drew tears from some
of them.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.180
Afterwards, the children's parents complained
to him about how hard he was hitting them

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:35.700
with the dodgeball.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.670
Sandler tried to defend himself with a solid
argument, but it didn't go as he planned.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:41.890
'I said, 'Didn't they read the script?'

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.090
They go, 'They're- They're six!

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:45.072
They don't read yet!'"

00:02:45.072 --> 00:02:49.329
Sandler promised not to do it again, but when
the cameras rolled he couldn't help himself

00:02:49.329 --> 00:02:51.650
and continued dishing out the punishment.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.900
As recalled by director Tamra Davis in a 2015
article published in The Washington Post -

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.290
"The kids started crying as soon as the ball
hit them, so I had to cut away before the

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:02.626
audience could see."

00:03:02.626 --> 00:03:08.069
Director Tamra Davis' involvement in the project
came about in an unusual way.

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:13.210
In 1994, Universal pegged her as its top choice
to helm "Billy Madison" and flew the young

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.370
director out to meet Sandler.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:20.080
Davis had helmed a comedy starring Chris Rock
called "CB4" for the studio a year earlier,

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.969
and the studio was so pleased with the job
she did that they were eager to work with

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:25.340
her once more.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:30.000
Writing about the meeting with Sandler in
an article for the Washington Post, Davis joked -

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.390
"I have a fondness for Jewish boys from New
York (I'm married to Mike D of the Beastie

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:38.739
Boys), and thought our meeting went well,
but later heard from the studio that Adam

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:41.190
wanted to work with an old friend."

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:45.090
According to Entertainment Weekly, that old
friend was Stephen Kessler, who had helped

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.329
Sandler early in his career by putting him
in a commercial.

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:53.480
However, two weeks into shooting, the studio
removed Kessler and brought Davis back.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.790
Sandler explained why in an interview with
Entertainment Weekly, saying -

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:01.519
"The studio wanted a less stylized movie,
more of me being a goof."

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:07.150
According to Davis, the movie was in serious
trouble, and Sandler was stressed out as a result.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:11.330
She immediately came in and changed the set's
atmosphere into a more relaxed environment,

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:14.115
allowing Sandler the freedom to do what he
does best.

00:04:14.115 --> 00:04:19.120
In the same Washington Post article, Davis
explained how she loosened up the set after

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.750
coming aboard as director.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:24.639
She only wanted smiling crew members eager
to laugh, which would prompt Sandler to do

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:25.821
his thing, writing -

00:04:25.821 --> 00:04:30.660
"I don't think [Universal] had any idea what
Adam's style of comedy really was when they

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.660
greenlighted the project — and neither did
I."

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:38.280
Sandler's style evidently involves a lot of
improvisation, which is why a majority of

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:42.310
the best gags in "Billy Madison" weren't included
in the original script.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:43.310
As Davis recalled -

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:47.890
"Many of the stranger but most frequently
quoted scenes in 'Billy Madison' were unplanned.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:52.919
I don't remember the 'shampoo is better' conversation
Adam has with himself being in the script,

00:04:52.919 --> 00:04:56.060
but that was what happened when Adam was loose
and having fun."

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:00.740
Other improvised bits include darker comedic
moments, such as when Doo Doo the Clown falls

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:05.110
down and hits his head — including the camera
panning down to show a trickle of blood coming

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:06.310
out of his mouth.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.940
Even the dodgeball scene was a last-second
addition.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:13.190
Thankfully, Davis was totally up for this
brand of comedy, otherwise "Billy Madison"

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.000
might have turned out quite differently.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.020
Fans of "Billy Madison" will recall the odd
side characters sprinkled throughout the film,

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:23.900
including Billy's first grade teacher with
a passion for interpretive dance, the accident

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.919
prone Doo Doo the Clown, and whoever Norm
MacDonald was supposed to be.

00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:33.009
Still, the best of the best remains Chris
Farley's angry, penguin-loving bus driver.

00:05:33.009 --> 00:05:38.020
Prone to violent, sweat-inducing rants, the
bus driver steals nearly every scene he is

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:41.350
in, mostly due to Farley's intense acting
techniques.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:45.630
According to an interview with director Tamra
Davis by Yahoo Entertainment, Farley drank

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:49.560
six shots of espresso to prepare for the scene,
with the director recalling -

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.889
"And then he went and did it, and he held
his breath until he turned red.

00:05:53.889 --> 00:05:57.979
Everybody else is laughing and I'm gonna have
a heart attack, because I'm like the mom […] I'm

00:05:57.979 --> 00:05:59.870
worried he's gonna have a heart attack."

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:04.160
Davis added that just as he did with his work
on 'Saturday Night Live,' Farley was willing

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.560
to push himself as far as he possibly could
for a laugh.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:08.668
"Everybody on?

00:06:08.668 --> 00:06:11.211
Good, great, grand, wonderful!

00:06:11.211 --> 00:06:14.306
NO YELLING ON THE BUS!"

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:18.740
Despite the excellent results that came about
from his intense methods, Davis remembered

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:19.740
herself thinking -

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.487
"Oh my God, you don't have to go that far!"

00:06:22.487 --> 00:06:27.250
Another nice touch in "Billy Madison" is the
inclusion of character actor Larry Hankin,

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:30.759
who appears as Carl Alphonse, Eric Gordon's
right-hand assistant.

00:06:30.759 --> 00:06:36.009
He's quiet, relatively cool, and taken more
by Billy's all-around nice-guy persona than

00:06:36.009 --> 00:06:38.220
Eric's break-the-little-guy attitude.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.460
About midway through the film, he approaches
Billy and warns him of Eric's treachery, adding

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:44.960
a little impersonation of Eric for good measure.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:49.080
Funnily enough, Hankin was apprehensive about
displaying his comedy chops.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:54.379
As revealed on the podcast 'Beyond the Backlot',
Hankin was initially hired by the film's original

00:06:54.379 --> 00:06:55.979
director, Stephen Kessler.

00:06:55.979 --> 00:07:01.020
After the first two or three days of filming,
Hankin was having lunch when Sandler called him over.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.020
As Hankin recalled on the podcast -

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:03.199
"He's questioning me.

00:07:03.199 --> 00:07:07.169
You know, how long have you been in show business
[…] and then [Sandler] said, 'Do you do

00:07:07.169 --> 00:07:08.599
anything funny?'"

00:07:08.599 --> 00:07:12.259
Taken aback by the comment, Hankin replied
that he had a funny face that he made for

00:07:12.259 --> 00:07:15.360
children but was too stupid to do for Sandler.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:20.000
However, Sandler pressed and the actor obliged
with the goofy face and laugh you see in the movie.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.982
Luckily, it makes Carl all the more lovable.

00:07:22.982 --> 00:07:28.229
Perhaps the greatest gift "Billy Madison"
bestowed upon audiences was the original Adam

00:07:28.229 --> 00:07:29.449
Sandler-Steve Buscemi team-up.

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:33.970
Although Buscemi's part in the comedy is minor,
it's incredibly memorable.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.280
He plays a former classmate of Billy's who
spends his free time sampling lipstick and

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.500
hunting the bullies of his youth, and the
gig paved the way for future roles in a number

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:43.780
of Sandler films.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:47.500
"Man, I'm glad I called that guy."

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:53.580
Still, despite memorable parts in everything
from "The Wedding Singer" to "Hubie Halloween,"

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.479
Buscemi actually prefers "Billy Madison" over
all else.

00:07:56.479 --> 00:08:00.409
The actor revealed as much during a Reddit
Q&amp;A back in 2015.

00:08:00.409 --> 00:08:04.830
A fan asked Buscemi which of his Sandler films
was his favorite, and the great character

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:05.830
actor replied -

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:10.120
"Well, I'm very partial to 'Billy Madison,'
because that was one of the first films Adam

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:13.000
did on his own, as part of his production
company."

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.680
He went on to discuss meeting Sandler on "Airheads,"
where the two actors had a great time working

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.120
alongside one another, leading to the aforementioned
long-running partnership.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:23.870
Buscemi closed out his answer by stating -

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:28.550
"I don't know if I have a favorite, but I
do really love the character in 'Billy Madison.'"

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.750
The world lost a legendary funnyman when Norm
Macdonald passed away.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:36.970
The man knew comedy better than most and had
a particular brand of humor that just worked

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:38.950
— provided you understood it.

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:43.729
That made him an obvious co-star for any Sandler
vehicle: the pair shared the screen in a number

00:08:43.729 --> 00:08:48.470
of projects and enjoyed a decades-long friendship
that continued until Macdonald's death in

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.339
September of 2021.

00:08:50.339 --> 00:08:55.040
Still, their greatest collaboration remains
"Billy Madison," where Macdonald had a bit

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.120
part as one of Billy's slacker friends who
spends far too much time getting drunk by

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:00.329
the pool.

00:09:00.329 --> 00:09:04.260
Sandler recalled the experience on "Conan,"
with Macdonald and a handful of his comedian

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:07.740
buddies, adding his own colorful commentary
for good measure.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:11.670
Apparently, in one of the pool scenes sprinkled
throughout "Billy Madison," Sandler's character

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.940
is supposed to speak with Macdonald's character,
Frank.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:18.010
Sandler said his line and was surprised when
Macdonald remained quiet.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:22.410
At first thinking that Norm was taking his
time before delivering his line, Sandler realized

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:23.750
that wasn't the case, saying -

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.240
"After a few more seconds, I noticed ole Norm
was sleeping."

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:31.410
Macdonald chimed in and joked that since he
was playing a drunk in the film, he had asked

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:34.390
for some booze so that he could do some method
acting for the scene.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.690
He followed that up with another ridiculous
explanation.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:42.593
"Also he called me Frank, and in my mind I'm
like, 'Who the f— is Frank?'"

00:09:42.593 --> 00:09:45.174
Only Norm Macdonald could get away with that
one.

00:09:45.174 --> 00:09:51.300
"Billy Madison" fans will likely recall the
O'Doyle clan that tormented Billy in every grade.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:54.970
These red-headed, mean-spirited bullies with
a hilariously simple catchphrase pick on Billy

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:59.670
by dumping chowder on his head and stuffing
his locker with horse manure before the entire

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:01.375
family meets a violent end.

00:10:01.375 --> 00:10:04.089
"O'Doyle rules!

00:10:04.089 --> 00:10:05.843
O'Doyle rules!

00:10:05.843 --> 00:10:07.534
O'Doyle rules!

00:10:07.534 --> 00:10:10.500
O'Doyle rules!"

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.930
This part of the movie can mostly be attributed
to Rich Wilkes.

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:18.470
Wilkes wrote "Airheads" and, according to
Vice, was hired by producer Robert Simonds

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:22.990
to do an uncredited rewrite of Sandler and
Tim Herlihy's "Billy Madison" script.

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:27.370
Feeling that much of the comedy seemed too
episodic, he suggested several changes to

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:28.640
straighten the plot out.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:33.060
For example, the original script called for
a young bully to frequently appear and torment

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:34.470
Billy with a slingshot.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:38.620
While Sandler and Herlihy came up with the
"O'Doyle rules" tagline, Wilkes was the one

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.950
who came up with their name in a rather unexpected
way, saying -

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:46.360
"I took the name from Brian Doyle-Murray,
Bill Murray's brother, because he's one of

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:47.649
the funniest guys ever.

00:10:47.649 --> 00:10:51.603
I added the 'O' because you want the O'Doyles
to be instantly recognizable — that's why

00:10:51.603 --> 00:10:52.820
they're all redheads."

